{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Let's try to find the limit using direct substitution.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{8-x}\u0026=\\dfrac{((-5)+1)^2}{8-(-5)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{(-4)^2}{13}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{16}{13}\n\\end{align}$\n\nWe got a finite number.\n\nSince $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{8-x}$ is continuous across its domain, we can determine that $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{8-x}$ is indeed equal to $\\dfrac{16}{13}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{8-x}=\\dfrac{16}{13}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{8-x}$.**\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]\n\n","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$\\dfrac{16}{13}$","correct":true},{"content":"$2$","correct":false},{"content":"$12$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"content":"The limit doesn't exist","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"randomize":false},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
